TI  - A Practical Analysis of Large Language Models Empowering Investment Management from the Financial Text Perspective
AB  - This paper investigates how large language models can assist in investment management through the processing of financial text. It is a discussion of ideas and applications, not an empirical investigation, and no original datasets, backtests, portfolios or performance data have been provided. The development path from dictionary-based textual indicators to Transformers, domain-adapted language models and retrieval-assisted workflows is presented. Next, it presents some applications of research-intake systems, document comparison, event extraction, risk monitoring, investment-memo drafting and client communication. Pay attention to the evidence supporting the results of the model. Although the generated summary and classification can increase the efficiency of the workflow, they are not verified facts, forecasts or investment recommendations unless supported by official data and professional reviews. A controlled operating model based on source provenance, retrieval, structured prompts, numerical verification, human approval, access control, audit log and continuous evaluation is proposed in this paper. Therefore, large language models are more suitable for the applications of supervised analysis interfaces than for autonomous portfolio management systems.
