TI  - Generative AI Advisory Chatbots and Customer Trust in Banking: Cognitive and Emotional Pathways and the Role of Human Handoff
AB  - Purpose – Banks are moving generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) chatbots from answering routine queries to giving personalized financial guidance. This shift raises a central question: when will customers trust, and act on, advice from a machine that can be wrong? Drawing on the stimulus–organism–response (SOR) framework, this conceptual paper develops a model of how GenAI advisory chatbots shape customers' willingness to accept their advice. Design/methodology/approach – The paper integrates recent (2025) evidence from marketing, information systems, finance and human–computer interaction research to build a theory-driven model and a set of testable propositions. Findings – The model proposes two trust routes. Perceived advice quality builds cognitive trust (confidence in the chatbot's competence), and perceived empathy builds emotional trust (a feeling of security and care). Both forms of trust increase advice acceptance. The availability of a human handoff, meaning the option to escalate to a human adviser, is proposed to strengthen both routes by acting as a safety net. Six propositions formalize these relationships. Research limitations/implications – The propositions require empirical testing. The paper sets out a scenario-based experimental design, a complementary field survey and recommended measures for doing so. Practical implications – For banks, chatbot accuracy alone is not enough. Customers also need to feel understood, and they need a visible route to a human when stakes are high. Designing for calibrated trust, rather than maximum trust, protects both customers and banks in a regulated industry. Originality/value – The paper is one of the few to separate cognitive and emotional trust in generative advisory chatbots in banking, and it positions human handoff as a design lever that shapes whether trust turns into action. Keywords: generative AI; chatbots; banking; financial advice; cognitive trust; emotional trust; human handoff; stimulus–organism–response; conceptual paper
