TI  - Cross-Cultural Patterns and Algorithm Appreciation in Multilingual LLM-Investor Conversations: Pre-Registration of Confirmatory Hypotheses
AB  - This pre-registration accompanies an empirical paper analysing 2,347 multilingual retail-investor LLM-conversation chats from an anonymised European retail-investor AI platform (March–October 2025). The dataset has been observed in full prior to registration; this is therefore a post-data-access registration that locks the analytical hypotheses, statistical tests, and decision rules for future replication and for transparent disclosure of the analysis path that produced the reported findings. We register these analyses honestly as exploratory analyses accompanied by preregistered confirmatory tests. We pre-register seven confirmatory hypotheses concerning: (1) classical home bias in conversational AI data, (2) cross-border culturally-proximate concentration, (3) defence-sector political-economy salience, (4) cross-cultural risk-language asymmetry, (5) financial-literacy proxy via ticker usage, (6) algorithm-appreciation evidence (low disagreement-language rate), and (7) an AI verbosity paradox. We additionally pre-register the inter-rater reliability methodology (independent LLM second-coder approach with a random 200-chat sample, seed=42, Cohen's kappa computation) and the planned 50-chat human spot-check for the camera-ready version.
